  "verbalization": "Osugacestat is implicated in the regulation of VMRC-MELG through a series of molecular interactions involving T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3 and other enzymes. The evidence suggests that Osugacestat acts as a target for multiple compounds, including 2-(1a,4-dimethyl-3,3a,4,5,6,7,7a,7b-octahydro-2H-naphtho[1,2-b]oxiren-7-yl)prop-2-enoic acid and Lup-20(29)-en-3-ol, (3beta)-, which both target VMRC-MELG. This indicates that Osugacestat may exert its effects through a common pathway, possibly by modulating the activity of CD3 or other enzymes involved in T-cell signaling. The presence of multiple targets suggests that Osugacestat may influence multiple biological processes, potentially through a complex network of interactions. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Osugacestat could modulate VMRC-MELG through a mechanism involving T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3 and related enzymes, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact mechanism and specificity.",
